ilya2@rcn.com wrote:
> Does vampire lord need to eat?

"Pet" vampires don't eat, but at least in wizmode, player vampires get
hungry. Slash'em has special code for vampires feeding on live
creatures and draining blood from fresh corpses, but in vanilla nethack
you can eat whatever you'd like. On a somewhat related note, vampires
regenerate.

> Does he continue accumulating experience -- that applies to any
> polymorphed form?

Yes. Stat changes are lost whenever you change forms, though. That may
be a good or bad thing, depending on what happened.

> I know he can not be affected by death wand or level draining -- what
> about paralysis, sickness and other special attacks?

Vampires are also immune to poison and sleep attacks. They are not
resistant to sickness attacks. This seems inconsistant with being
undead, but those are the breaks.

Vampires are silver-hating. Silver projectiles will do extra (d20)
damage, and abuse your cons. However, by my reading of the source, it
appears that this check has been overlooked for melee weapons. This
would seem to be a bug, but as far as I can tell, player vampires don't
take any extra damage from silver melee weapons. Silver weapons and
artifacts can be safely wielded, subject to the usual artifact blasting
rules. Grayswandir won't blast you as long as you're lawful, but the
Sunsword and Mitre of Holiness will blast you if wielded/worn,
regardless of your class and alignment.

> Can vampire lord drown? Or is it something more subtle -- "vampires and
> running water" thing?

Vampires have innate levitation, which makes it difficult to enter
water. Never the less, they don't breath, so they can't drown or choke
to death. Eels can wrap themselves around you, but the attack has no
effect. It doesn't even appear to get you wet. Note that this is due to
the unbreathing property, *not* the levitation.

If you ever wish to voluntarily enter the water, the only way I know of
is to be chained to an iron ball. Reading a scroll of punishment while
levitating over water produces an odd message combination: "You are
being punished for your misbehavior!  Splash!  Splash!"

> Not directly related to my character's new form: will eating a tin of
> acid blob stop stoning? Or is the process of weilding a tin opener,
> then opening a tin, then eating it so slow I would turn to stone before
> it is complete?

Tins are just a potent as corpses, but they do take longer. Stoning
takes 5 turns. Movement speed may affect how much you can actually do
during that time. The advantage of lizard corpses is two-fold: they
help prevent stoning in the first place, and eating (or even just
*starting* to eat) them will cure stoning. As an added bonus, if you
get interrupted, the leftover lizard can be used to cure stoning again.

A blessed tin takes 1 turn to open (which *can* be interrupted), and 1
turn to eat. That's not too shabby, but it's still not as sure-fire as
a lizard corpse. The tin opener maneuver is possible, taking 3 turns to
get it eaten if you aren't interrupted. It wouldn't be my prefered
method, but I might try it if I were desperate. It's also possible to
do this with any dagger, athame, or crysknife, but the whole process
takes exactly 5 turns if not interrupted. I would only try it as a last
resort.



